{
 "idea": {
  "id": "short_slow",
  "family": "short-flow",
  "title": "Short-sale pressure as a slow signal: weeks of heavy shorting, held five sessions",
  "why": "`short_pressure` found that a day of unusually heavy shorting comes before weak returns (0.7-1.05 before costs) but the daily ranking replaced the whole book every day and costs took all of it. If short sellers are informed about something that takes weeks to come out (Boehmer, Jones and Zhang find the effect lasts 20 sessions and more), a 10- or 20-day average of the short share against the stock's own earlier 120 sessions should carry the same information and change slowly, so a book held five sessions and rolled open to open keeps most of its names and pays a fraction of the cost. The warning sign is already in the ledger: the 5-day average had no before-cost return. If the slow versions are empty too, the information is in the single day and cannot be traded at these costs. `level20` drops the own-norm (Boehmer's sort is on the level). `short_only` tests the side the story is about. `spike_short` is the other way to cut trading: only the most extreme single days, where an informed seller should show most clearly, each paying its own round trip. The data starts in August 2018, so discovery is three years and a t of 3 is out of reach; this run can kill the direction or keep it alive, not pass it.",
  "source": "Boehmer, Jones, Zhang 2008; Diether, Lee, Werner 2009; lab session 2026-11-22 (short_pressure)",
  "market": "us",
